WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.400
"Eternals" is one of Marvel&nbsp;
Studios' most ambitious films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.520
It's an origin story for&nbsp;
almost a dozen characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:13.920
and introduces a ton of intergalactic lore. With&nbsp;
so much setup, it's no surprise there are some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:19.040
things left unanswered. These are the biggest&nbsp;
questions we have at the end of "Eternals."

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.400
The movie's climax sees the Eternals joining&nbsp;
powers and stopping the Celestial Tiamut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:28.080
from destroying the Earth. After&nbsp;
some infighting and trepidation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.480
they all work together and petrify him&nbsp;
just as he's emerging from the sea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:36.240
It's a happy ending for humanity&nbsp;
-- not so much for the Eternals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:41.760
who now have to answer for sabotaging their&nbsp;
own mission, but certainly for humanity. Right?

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:47.840
Well, happy, yes. An ending? Not so much.&nbsp;
When the Eternals discussed their plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:52.320
it was clear that they were only planning on&nbsp;
freezing the Celestial. It doesn't seem like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:58.080
that plan changed, and the fact that his body&nbsp;
is still undamaged certainly implies he could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:03.200
still be alive. Will the Eternals be there when&nbsp;
he breaks free? Will they find a way to extract&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.320
him without severely damaging the planet?&nbsp;
Time will hopefully tell -- especially if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:13.280
humanity isn't deemed worthy enough once&nbsp;
Arishem examines the Eternals' memories.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:18.720
Even if he's dead, though, there's still a&nbsp;
frozen Celestial emerging from the planet's core.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.520
If nothing else, that's going to ruin more&nbsp;
than a few days somewhere in the world.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.560
Dane Whitman only has a handful&nbsp;
of scenes in "Eternals," a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:32.800
surprise given that he's portrayed by&nbsp;
an actor as prominent as Kit Harington.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:38.000
Even so, his scenes are of great&nbsp;
narrative and emotional importance.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.400
In his final scene before the credits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.400
Dane starts telling Sersi that his family&nbsp;
history was more complicated than he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:50.480
thought -- only to be so rudely interrupted&nbsp;
by Arishem literally stealing his girl.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:55.040
The post-credits scene, however, teases&nbsp;
Dane's future. We see him approach a sword,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:59.360
only to be asked by an unseen&nbsp;
voice if he wants to do that.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:04.480
Comics fans will at least partly see where this&nbsp;
is going: Dane Whitman is the Black Knight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:10.400
a superhero who wields a powerful and mystical&nbsp;
sword. He's often portrayed as a descendant of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.800
the original Black Knight, who appeared&nbsp;
in various adventure comics from Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:20.720
pre-Stan Lee. He's also the nephew of another,&nbsp;
villainous Black Knight -- and if the movies stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:26.000
true to the comics, that's likely the uncle with&nbsp;
whom Sersi said Dane had a strained relationship.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:26.472
"Introducing the Eternals,"

00:02:26.472 --> 00:02:29.120
"You hear that, Sprite? Family reunion."

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:29.920
"It's about time."

00:02:30.667 --> 00:02:35.867
Ikaris is among the most thoroughly developed&nbsp;
members of the Eternals, and in the ensemble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.867 --> 00:02:41.147
he's arguably one of the two or three&nbsp;
main characters. Partly because of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.147 --> 00:02:46.187
his future is pregnant with more&nbsp;
possibilities than anyone else on the team.

00:02:46.187 --> 00:02:47.707
"Who do you think's gonna lead the Avengers?"

00:02:49.867 --> 00:02:52.027
"I could lead them."

00:02:56.107 --> 00:03:00.107
Originally hellbent on releasing the&nbsp;
latent Celestial from earth's crust,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.107 --> 00:03:03.867
his love of Sersi wins out&nbsp;
and he rethinks his mission.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.867 --> 00:03:08.187
When we last see him, he's flying&nbsp;
far away from Earth, into a star.

00:03:08.187 --> 00:03:14.027
The obvious question is: what next? We didn't see&nbsp;
him when Arishem came back to Earth, and we didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.027 --> 00:03:19.867
see him on the ship in the post-credits scene.&nbsp;
He's almost certainly still out there, but where?

00:03:19.867 --> 00:03:24.267
The two most likely possibilities are that&nbsp;
he informed Arishem himself of what happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.267 --> 00:03:29.307
likely seeking some kind of atonement, or that&nbsp;
he went into hiding from Arishem entirely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.307 --> 00:03:34.587
Either way, we'll almost certainly see&nbsp;
him again and find out what happens next.

00:03:35.147 --> 00:03:41.067
Early in the movie, Dane asks Sersi the question&nbsp;
we've all had since "Eternals" was announced: why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.067 --> 00:03:47.147
did they stay out of various human conflicts, up&nbsp;
to and including the one with Thanos? The answer:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.147 --> 00:03:52.187
the Eternals were explicitly instructed not&nbsp;
to get involved with any human affairs that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.187 --> 00:03:57.147
didn't involve the Deviants. Okay, fair&nbsp;
enough. That raises another question:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.147 --> 00:04:00.667
what about all that other&nbsp;
interfering they definitely did?

00:04:00.667 --> 00:04:04.907
As an example, we see the Eternals protect&nbsp;
the residents of Babylon from the Deviants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.907 --> 00:04:10.027
and it can be inferred that the Walls of Babylon&nbsp;
were meant to protect humans from Deviants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.027 --> 00:04:14.587
But the Eternals were far more than mere&nbsp;
protection for the Babylonians -- they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.587 --> 00:04:19.787
were the most important and influential&nbsp;
beings there. They provided leadership,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.787 --> 00:04:24.187
guidance, and entertainment for the&nbsp;
comparatively primitive culture.

00:04:24.187 --> 00:04:28.187
We do eventually find out that the&nbsp;
Eternals fight off the Deviants entirely so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.187 --> 00:04:32.907
their planet will develop the requisite amount&nbsp;
of intelligent life to incubate a Celestial.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.907 --> 00:04:36.987
This explains some of it, like how&nbsp;
Phastos developed farming equipment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.987 --> 00:04:41.707
But Phastos didn't know about the true&nbsp;
purpose of the mission until much later.

00:04:41.707 --> 00:04:46.027
If nothing else, surely they would have&nbsp;
realized over the course of centuries that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.027 --> 00:04:49.467
finite resources often lead&nbsp;
to conflict. Either way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.467 --> 00:04:54.347
they sure interfered a lot for people&nbsp;
who weren't supposed to interfere.

00:04:55.147 --> 00:05:00.587
Sersi's exact powers are a bit nebulous, but&nbsp;
one thing that's clear is that she's able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.587 --> 00:05:05.947
manipulate and transform inanimate matter. This&nbsp;
mostly takes the form of changing something's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.947 --> 00:05:09.867
density or turning one form of matter&nbsp;
into another. Halfway through the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.867 --> 00:05:15.867
she surprises herself with a new power: she&nbsp;
somehow turns a Deviant into a tree. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.867 --> 00:05:21.787
a major turning point in the movie, one that sets&nbsp;
up the climactic attempt to freeze the Celestial.

00:05:21.787 --> 00:05:25.147
It's also never explained how she got that power.

00:05:25.147 --> 00:05:30.667
At one point, she's asked point blank when it&nbsp;
happened. She responds that she doesn't know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.667 --> 00:05:34.187
in a tone that shows she's&nbsp;
clearly baffled herself.

00:05:34.187 --> 00:05:39.707
At no point in the film's runtime is it ever&nbsp;
explained exactly how she developed this power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.707 --> 00:05:44.027
or how long she's had it. It's possible&nbsp;
that becoming the leader of the Eternals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.027 --> 00:05:48.827
after Ajak's death contributed to&nbsp;
it, but that's never mentioned nor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.827 --> 00:05:55.467
explored. The power is plot critical, but the&nbsp;
reason she has that power remains a mystery.

00:05:56.267 --> 00:06:01.211
Much to the chagrin of many moviegoers and&nbsp;
Marvel's PR department, the "Eternals" post&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.211 --> 00:06:04.347
credits scene was leaked by a few&nbsp;
members of the west coast press:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.347 --> 00:06:10.027
Harry Styles has joined the MCU as Eros,&nbsp;
Thanos' handsome and charismatic brother.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.027 --> 00:06:15.307
Marvel tried very hard to hide this, going&nbsp;
so far as to cut both post credits scenes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.307 --> 00:06:18.987
the credits themselves from screenings&nbsp;
outside of the Los Angeles premiere.

00:06:18.987 --> 00:06:23.467
Eros, also known as Starfox, is far&nbsp;
less brutal than his brother in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.467 --> 00:06:28.987
comics. That's not to say he's a good guy,&nbsp;
he's just not a universe-conquering titan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.987 --> 00:06:33.067
and can alternate between hero or&nbsp;
villain depending on the story.

00:06:33.067 --> 00:06:38.267
Eros' main power, besides the requisite&nbsp;
ability to shoot various beams, is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.267 --> 00:06:44.107
of emotional manipulation: he can form psychic&nbsp;
connections with people and change how they feel.

00:06:44.107 --> 00:06:48.507
There are several questions here, but&nbsp;
they all amount to the same thing:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.507 --> 00:06:54.427
what's he going to do in the MCU? Will he be the&nbsp;
next big villain? Will the upcoming "She-Hulk" on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.427 --> 00:06:59.547
Disney+ adapt the storyline in which he goes&nbsp;
on trial for manipulating women's feelings?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.547 --> 00:07:04.027
Perhaps most importantly, will&nbsp;
he sing? Please let him sing.

00:07:04.747 --> 00:07:07.787
For half of "Eternals," we're led&nbsp;
to believe that the Deviants are an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.787 --> 00:07:12.267
evil alien force that indiscriminately&nbsp;
attacks life on various planets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.267 --> 00:07:17.547
We're given no reason to doubt that and see the&nbsp;
Eternals -- who were supposedly created by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.547 --> 00:07:22.747
Celestials to stop the Deviants -- fight them&nbsp;
off over the course of countless generations.

00:07:22.747 --> 00:07:27.387
Then, about halfway into the movie, we're&nbsp;
thrown a curveball: the Celestials created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.387 --> 00:07:32.347
the Deviants first, as a way to control&nbsp;
environments with no natural apex predators.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.347 --> 00:07:37.467
They only became bloodthirsty after a flaw in&nbsp;
their design saw them evolve out of control.

00:07:37.467 --> 00:07:39.707
At least one Deviant learns to talk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.707 --> 00:07:45.787
and confirms this story to the Eternals. But&nbsp;
that raises the question: what was the flaw?

00:07:45.787 --> 00:07:51.387
This is admittedly getting into the weeds a bit,&nbsp;
but "Eternals" is a movie packed to the gills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.387 --> 00:07:56.347
with detailed lore. Will we ever learn about&nbsp;
the Deviants' true design, or what went wrong?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.347 --> 00:08:02.027
Are the Deviants themselves aware of this flaw?&nbsp;
Can it be corrected? Given that we haven't seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.027 --> 00:08:07.227
the last of the Deviants on Earth and have&nbsp;
barely seen them on any other planets at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.227 --> 00:08:12.027
we might find out one day. Or it'll never&nbsp;
be brought up again. Place your bets.

